TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior


Found by the B064 lane while fixing that ticket. NOT part of B064 and
deliberately left alone there; filed so it is tracked rather than rediscovered.

## Defect

`TypeIndex::load_with_header` (src/typedb/index.rs) reads the two databases in
two separate steps:

    let core  = CoreDatabase::load_from_file(core_path)?;    // read 1
    index.add_core(&core);
    let nadeo = NadeoDatabase::load_from_file(nadeo_path)?;  // read 2
    index.add_nadeo(&nadeo);

Nothing holds the pair still between them. If the Openplanet dump set is
regenerated in that window -- which the game does, and which is the entire
reason `openplanet.regenerateTypeDb` exists -- the resulting index carries the
Core half of one database and the game half of another. No concurrency in the
LSP is needed: the writer is the game, an unrelated process.

Same SHAPE as B064's defect (b), one level down, and NOT fixed by B064. B064
made the stamps come out of the parse, so a mixed load now honestly reports
`core_op` from one database and `game_op` from the other rather than silently
naming one. Honest, but still a chimera index.

## Severity: low, and be honest about why

The window is the game-dump parse, so tens of milliseconds, and it needs the
game to rewrite the dump inside it. The consequence is a type surface that
never existed as a shipped pair -- most likely spurious "no member" diagnostics
across the Core/engine boundary. Nobody has reported it; it was reasoned to,
not observed.

## Work (sketch, not prescribed)

Read both files' BYTES first and parse from those, so the two reads are as
close together as they can be; or detect the split afterwards (stat both files
before and after, re-read if either moved). Neither is free and neither is
airtight -- worth weighing the options before picking one.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

tests/b064_typedb_atomicity.rs already has the machinery: `write_core` /
`write_game` write distinguishable databases atomically via rename, and
`Version` makes "which database is this" observable from the symbol count. A
red test rewrites the GAME dump after the Core read and asserts the loaded
index is not a mix. Note that file deliberately holds the game dump CONSTANT
across its two versions precisely to keep this defect out of B064's tests; see
the comment on `GAME_OP`.

## Related

B064 (fixed the stamps and the lock split; this is the load's own read split).

