## Correction from the lane (2026-08-31)

Two errors in my own filing, both found by building the thing.

**It is three reads, not two.** The body says "reads its two JSON files
separately". `TypeIndex::load` makes THREE `read_to_string` calls, and the one
I missed is the earliest:

    load():               HeaderDatabase::load_from_file(Openplanet.h)   <- read 1
      load_with_header():   CoreDatabase::load_from_file(...Core.json)   <- read 2
                            index.add_core(&core)          <- the widest gap
                            NadeoDatabase::load_from_file(...Next.json)  <- read 3

so the chimera can span three generations, not two, and `Openplanet.h` is a
first-class member of the set rather than an afterthought. Measured, not
reasoned: rewriting the dump after read 2 produced `core: A, game: A,
header: B`.

**The Work sketch proposes the half that does not work.** "Read both files'
BYTES first, so the two reads are as close together as they can be" was
measured as a standalone change and left 6 of 9 tests red. Narrowing the window
closes nothing, because the window is a position in a sequence rather than a
duration and the writer is an unrelated process that can land anywhere in it.
What closes the defect is stamping each file's identity before and after the
reads and re-reading a set that moved - with narrowing kept as defence in
depth, and because it is what lets a torn read be caught before anything is
parsed. Anyone picking this up from the sketch alone would have shipped the
wrong half.

## Coordinator note (2026-08-31)

Appended verbatim at the lane's request. The half-fix matrix behind it:

| variant | red |
|---|---|
| narrow only (bytes up front, no check) - the ticket's sketch | 6 / 9 |
| reject only (identity check, read span left wide) | 0 / 9 |
| both (shipped) | 0 / 9 |

This is the SECOND ticket in this batch whose own suggested fix was the half
that does nothing - B064's (a) was the other. Both were caught by measuring
each half as a standalone variant rather than by reading the code. Treat a
ticket's Work section as a hypothesis to be measured, not as a specification.